Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 03/16/2023]
1. Strengths of resonances above Er=500 keV are adopted from Endt 1998, but renormalized to standard value for Er=403 keV listed in Sargood 1982. When no strength 
   uncertainties are reported in Endt 1998, we assumed 25%.
2. Er=214.7 keV: proton width from reported spectroscopic factor in Dykoski et al. combined with upper limit derived in the present work; value of Gg=1.0 eV is a 
   guess (inconsequential).
3. Er=121.7 keV: proton width for assumption 5+ (ell=4) estimated from Fig. 2 of Dykoski et al. Since 7+ (ell=6) gives an even smaller value, we treat the listed 
   proton partial width as an upper limit; value of Gg=1.0 eV is a guess (inconsequential).
4. Er=108.0 keV: proton width upper limit for ell=0 estimated from Fig. 2 of Dykoski et al. Gamma-ray partial width from measured mean lifetime in Tikkanen et al.
5. Mean reduced width values estimated from Fig. 4 of Pogrebnyak et al. (2013); we assume a median [recommended] value of 1.0e-3; the value "-5.0" indicates a 
   factor uncertainty of 5, assuming a lognormal probability density.
6. Resonance energies: calculated from the listed excitation energies using the Q value derived from nuclear masses.
7. Direct capture calculated using experimental C2S values from Dykoski & Dehnhard (1976), Endt (1977), and Vernotte et al. (1994).
8. Resonance strengths of Er=303 and 314 keV from direct measurement of Downen et al. (2022).
9. Contribution from -87 keV subthreshold resonance is negligible compared to DC.

